WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:17.000
Oxford Bookworms Fact Files.
London.
by John Escott.
read by Judy Bennett.
published and copyright
Oxford University Press 2000.

00:30.000 --> 00:38.950
Chapter 1

00:39.000 --> 00:43.950
This is London.

00:44.000 --> 00:51.950
Every year, more than 9 million people come from countries all over the world

00:52.000 --> 00:54.950
to visit London.

00:55.000 --> 00:59.950
They go to the theatres and museums.

01:00.000 --> 01:07.950
They look at interesting old buildings, many of them hundreds of years old.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.950
They sit or walk in the beautiful parks

01:13.000 --> 01:16.950
or have a drink in a pub.

01:17.000 --> 01:24.950
They go to Oxford Street and look at the shops or to Harrods.

01:25.000 --> 01:29.950
Two million visitors go to the Tower of London.

01:30.000 --> 01:36.950
A million more go to see St Paul's Cathedral.

01:37.000 --> 01:42.950
Yes, London is a big and beautiful city

01:43.000 --> 01:46.950
with lots to see and do.

01:47.000 --> 01:50.000
How did it all begin?

02:10.000 --> 02:11.950
Chapter 2

02:12.000 --> 02:14.950
In the Beginning

02:15.000 --> 02:18.950
The name London comes from the Romans.

02:19.000 --> 02:22.950
There were people living here before they came,

02:23.000 --> 02:27.950
but we do not know very much about them.

02:28.000 --> 02:32.950
The Romans came to England in AD 43.

02:33.000 --> 02:39.950
They built houses and other buildings and made a town next to the River Thames.

02:40.000 --> 02:44.000
They called the town Londonium.

02:45.000 --> 02:52.950
They built a bridge over the river and ships came up to Londonium from the sea.

02:53.000 --> 02:56.950
The town got bigger and bigger.

02:57.000 --> 03:04.950
Important new buildings went up and you can see some of the Roman city wall today

03:05.000 --> 03:09.000
near the Museum of London.

03:10.000 --> 03:15.950
It was a rich town with about 50,000 people living in it.

03:16.000 --> 03:19.950
But soon after AD 400,

03:20.000 --> 03:24.950
the Romans left Londonium to go back to Rome

03:25.000 --> 03:29.950
and nobody lived in the town for many hundreds of years.

03:30.000 --> 03:33.950
The buildings began to fall down.

03:34.000 --> 03:41.950
Danish soldiers destroyed more buildings nearly 500 years later.

03:42.000 --> 03:45.950
King Alfred was king of England then.

03:46.000 --> 03:53.950
He got the Danes to leave London and his men built the town again.

03:54.000 --> 04:01.950
In 1066, William the Conqueror came to England from France to be king.

04:02.000 --> 04:08.950
Soon after, he began to build the Tower of London.

04:09.000 --> 04:13.950
When Henry VIII was king in 1509,

04:14.000 --> 04:18.950
50,000 people lived in London again.

04:19.000 --> 04:24.950
By the year 1600, there were 200,000,

04:25.000 --> 04:31.000
but a lot of them lived in old and dirty buildings.

04:32.000 --> 04:39.950
In 1665, 100,000 people died from an illness called the Plague.

04:40.000 --> 04:44.950
This was called the Year of the Great Plague.

04:45.000 --> 04:51.950
A year later, in 1666, there was a big fire,

04:52.000 --> 04:54.950
the fire of London.

04:55.000 --> 05:01.950
It began in a house in Pudding Lane, the London Bridge.

05:02.000 --> 05:08.950
More than a quarter of a million Londoners lost their homes in the fire.

05:09.000 --> 05:15.950
It destroyed St Paul's Cathedral and 88 other churches.

05:16.000 --> 05:20.950
But the fire also destroyed most of the worst old buildings,

05:21.000 --> 05:28.950
and the new houses that went up after this were better for people to live in.

05:29.000 --> 05:32.950
A new St Paul's Cathedral was built between

05:33.000 --> 05:37.950
1675 and 1711.

05:38.000 --> 05:43.950
By 1881, more than 3 million people lived in London.

05:44.000 --> 05:48.950
Today, more than 6 million people live here.

05:49.000 --> 05:56.950
There were 8 million in the 1960s, but in the 1970s and 1980s,

05:57.000 --> 06:00.950
people moved out of the centre of London.

06:01.000 --> 06:19.950
MUSIC

06:20.000 --> 06:21.950
Chapter 3

06:22.000 --> 06:24.950
Visiting the City

06:25.000 --> 06:30.950
A quick and easy way to get to different places in the city

06:31.000 --> 06:33.950
is to use an underground train.

06:34.000 --> 06:39.950
The trains run all day and most of the night.

06:40.000 --> 06:44.950
Buy your ticket before you get on the train.

06:45.000 --> 06:52.950
It's better not to make your journey between 8 o'clock and 10 o'clock in the morning

06:53.000 --> 06:57.950
or 4 o'clock and 6 o'clock in the evening.

06:58.000 --> 07:01.950
These are called the rush hours.

07:02.000 --> 07:07.950
Thousands of people are going to work or coming home again then,

07:08.000 --> 07:15.950
and it is difficult to move or to find a place to sit on the train.

07:16.000 --> 07:22.950
You can see much more of London from one of its famous red buses.

07:23.000 --> 07:30.950
Some special visitors' buses take you to many of the interesting places in the city

07:31.000 --> 07:32.950
on one journey.

07:33.000 --> 07:37.950
It takes about one and a half hours,

07:38.000 --> 07:41.950
but you can break your journey and get off

07:42.000 --> 07:48.000
and on again at the different places you want to visit.

07:49.000 --> 07:53.950
London taxis are called black cabs.

07:54.000 --> 07:59.950
Most of them are black, but some are not.

08:00.000 --> 08:06.950
You can stop one if it has a for hire sign on it.

08:07.000 --> 08:13.000
The drivers are usually friendly and helpful.

08:14.000 --> 08:18.950
Why not take a boat trip along the river Thames?

08:19.000 --> 08:25.950
Boats leave Westminster Pier and Charing Cross Pier,

08:26.000 --> 08:31.950
and they go to Tower Pier and Greenwich.

08:32.000 --> 08:39.950
Between April and October, you can take a longer boat trip to Hampton Court,

08:40.000 --> 08:45.950
about four hours, a beautiful palace in a big park.

08:46.000 --> 09:09.950
Chapter 4

09:10.000 --> 09:12.950
Some places to go.

09:13.000 --> 09:17.950
The Queen has her London home at Buckingham Palace.

09:18.000 --> 09:25.950
It is at the end of the Mall, a long road that begins at Trafalgar Square.

09:26.000 --> 09:29.950
At half past eleven most mornings,

09:30.000 --> 09:34.950
the soldiers at Buckingham Palace change the guard.

09:35.000 --> 09:41.950
It takes about thirty minutes, and hundreds of visitors come to watch.

09:42.000 --> 09:45.950
And in August and September,

09:46.000 --> 09:50.950
you can usually visit some of the rooms in the palace.

09:51.000 --> 09:58.950
But there are always lots of people coming to see them, so be ready to wait.

09:59.000 --> 10:04.950
You can also visit the Royal Muse at the palace.

10:05.000 --> 10:10.950
This is the home of the Queen's horses and coaches.

10:11.000 --> 10:14.950
The Queen's Gallery is also at the palace,

10:15.000 --> 10:19.950
and you can visit it at most times of the year.

10:20.000 --> 10:25.950
Here you can see pictures from all over the world.

10:26.000 --> 10:33.950
The Tower of London is now a museum, and one of London's most famous buildings.

10:34.000 --> 10:38.950
More than two million people visit it every year.

10:39.000 --> 10:43.950
Yeoman waters, also called beef eaters,

10:44.000 --> 10:48.950
tell them all about the tower.

10:49.000 --> 10:56.950
You can see the crown jewels, and visit the bloody tower and the white tower.

10:57.000 --> 11:04.950
Or take a walk round the wall, and perhaps see one of the tower's famous black birds,

11:05.000 --> 11:07.950
the ravens.

11:08.000 --> 11:11.950
Tower Bridge is near the Tower of London.

11:12.000 --> 11:16.950
It is one of the most famous bridges in the city,

11:17.000 --> 11:21.950
and first opened in 1894.

11:22.000 --> 11:28.950
St Paul's Cathedral is not far away on Ludget Hill.

11:29.000 --> 11:34.950
It was built by Sir Christopher Wren after the fire of London.

11:35.000 --> 11:40.950
Wren built more than 50 London churches.

11:41.000 --> 11:47.000
Visitors can go up to the Golden Gallery to look across London.

12:06.000 --> 12:10.950
Chapter 5 Westminster

12:11.000 --> 12:15.950
Westminster Abbey is more than 900 years old

12:16.000 --> 12:20.950
and is a very famous London church.

12:21.000 --> 12:23.950
After William the Conqueror,

12:24.000 --> 12:31.000
every king and queen of England was crowned king or queen here.

12:32.000 --> 12:36.950
The houses of Parliament are near Westminster Abbey.

12:37.000 --> 12:40.950
This is the home of the British government.

12:41.000 --> 12:47.950
The clock high up on the building is called Big Ben.

12:48.000 --> 12:55.950
The Prime Minister, the head of the British government, lives at 10 Downing Street.

12:56.000 --> 13:02.950
Walk along Parliament Street to Whitehall, past the government buildings,

13:03.000 --> 13:08.950
and you can see the little street on your left.

13:09.000 --> 13:12.950
Parks and Gardens

13:13.000 --> 13:16.950
When you are tired of looking at buildings,

13:17.000 --> 13:23.950
you can sit or walk in one of London's beautiful parks.

13:24.000 --> 13:29.000
Hyde Park has a lake in the middle called the Serpentine.

13:30.000 --> 13:33.950
And you can take a boat out on the water.

13:34.000 --> 13:41.950
It is a good place to get away from the crowds and the noise of the city.

13:42.000 --> 13:48.950
You can listen to the speakers at speakers corner near Marble Arch.

13:49.000 --> 13:53.950
People from all over the world come and speak here.

13:54.000 --> 13:58.950
You can ask a speaker some difficult questions if you like,

13:59.000 --> 14:05.950
or you can stand on a box and speak to some of the listeners.

14:06.000 --> 14:11.950
Kensington Gardens is next to Hyde Park.

14:12.000 --> 14:15.950
Here you can see the statue of Peter Pan,

14:16.000 --> 14:23.950
the famous boy in the children's story, Peter Pan, by J.M. Barrie.

14:24.000 --> 14:28.950
Regent's Park is the home of London Zoo.

14:29.000 --> 14:35.950
The zoo has thousands of birds and animals from all over the world.

14:36.000 --> 14:40.000
There is also a theatre in the park.

14:41.000 --> 14:48.950
On a summer's evening, you can sit out under the night sky and watch a play by William Shakespeare,

14:49.000 --> 14:52.950
England's most famous writer.

14:53.000 --> 14:56.950
St James's Park is next to the mall.

14:57.000 --> 15:03.000
It is smaller, but many people think it is more beautiful.

15:10.000 --> 15:23.950
Chapter 6

15:24.000 --> 15:28.950
Shops and Eating

15:29.000 --> 15:35.950
The most famous shop in London, some people say the most famous in the world,

15:36.000 --> 15:38.950
is Harrods in Knightsbridge.

15:39.000 --> 15:43.950
It opened in 1849.

15:44.000 --> 15:51.950
Oxford Street has many big shops, Selfridges, Marks and Spencer,

15:52.000 --> 15:56.950
John Lewis, Debenhams.

15:57.000 --> 16:01.950
There are always lots of people looking at the shops here,

16:02.000 --> 16:07.950
but at Christmas, thousands more people come to see the wonderful Christmas lights.

16:08.000 --> 16:13.950
And to buy things for their friends and family for Christmas.

16:14.000 --> 16:18.950
Charing Cross is famous for its book shops.

16:19.000 --> 16:24.950
There are lots of them, and they sell old and new books.

16:25.000 --> 16:30.950
One of the oldest and most famous is foils.

16:31.000 --> 16:33.950
It has thousands of books,

16:34.000 --> 16:41.950
but it can sometimes be very difficult to find the book you want.

16:42.000 --> 16:45.950
Covent Garden was once a big food market,

16:46.000 --> 16:50.950
but now it has lots of small shops and cafes,

16:51.000 --> 16:55.950
and there are street performers to watch.

16:56.000 --> 17:03.950
There are two very famous markets in London, Petticoat Lane Market, open on Sundays,

17:04.000 --> 17:06.950
is in Middlesex Street,

17:07.000 --> 17:13.950
and is a good place to buy cheap clothes and things for the home.

17:14.000 --> 17:21.950
At the market in Portobello Road, open on Saturdays, you can buy old clocks,

17:22.000 --> 17:28.950
old chairs and tables and hundreds of other things.

17:29.000 --> 17:34.950
You can find food from nearly every country in the world in London,

17:35.000 --> 17:38.950
in Soho, in the West End of London,

17:39.000 --> 17:43.950
C-Map on pages 18 and 19.

17:44.000 --> 17:49.950
You can eat food from Italy, India, China,

17:50.000 --> 17:55.950
Japan, Greece and lots more places.

17:56.000 --> 18:00.950
There are also thousands of pubs in the city.

18:01.000 --> 18:05.950
In many pubs, you can eat as well as drink.

18:06.000 --> 18:09.950
Or why not have some English fish and chips?

18:10.000 --> 18:13.950
They are cheap and good to eat.

18:14.000 --> 18:22.000
Or you can have tea at the rits in Piccadilly, or at the Savoy Hotel in the Strand.

18:26.000 --> 18:43.950
CHAPTER VII.

18:44.000 --> 18:46.950
GOING OUT.

18:47.000 --> 18:50.000
THEATERS AND MUSIC.

18:51.000 --> 18:55.950
London's West End has some of the best theatres in the world,

18:56.000 --> 18:59.950
so tickets can be expensive.

19:00.000 --> 19:03.950
Go in the afternoon, it is often cheaper.

19:04.000 --> 19:06.950
There is something for everybody.

19:07.000 --> 19:12.950
From a play by the Royal Shakespeare Company at the Barbican Theatre

19:13.000 --> 19:18.950
to Agatha Christie's The Mouse Trap, this play began in 1952,

19:19.000 --> 19:23.950
and thousands of visitors see it every year.

19:24.000 --> 19:26.950
There are lots of cinemas to visit.

19:27.000 --> 19:30.950
The most expensive are in the West End,

19:31.000 --> 19:35.950
but you can sometimes get cheap tickets on Mondays.

19:36.000 --> 19:40.950
You can hear wonderful music and singing from all over the world

19:41.000 --> 19:48.950
at the Royal Opera House in Covent Garden, the Royal Albert Hall in Kensington Gore,

19:49.000 --> 19:56.950
the London Coliseum in St Martin's Lane, and the Barbican Centre Silk Street.

19:57.000 --> 20:02.950
For ballet, go to the Sadler's Wells Theatre in Rosebury Avenue

20:03.000 --> 20:06.950
or to the Royal Opera House.

20:07.000 --> 20:13.950
To get cheap tickets by them an hour or two before it begins.

20:14.000 --> 20:17.950
Do you like to listen to jazz musicians?

20:18.000 --> 20:23.950
You can hear some of the best at Ronnie Scott's Club in Frith Street,

20:24.000 --> 20:28.950
or at the Bull's Head Barns at Barns Bridge,

20:29.000 --> 20:35.950
or at 100 Club at 100 Oxford Street.

20:36.000 --> 20:38.950
Sport.

20:39.000 --> 20:43.950
Some of the most famous English football clubs are in London.

20:44.000 --> 20:51.950
You can see Arsenal Play at Arsenal Stadium, Avernal Road, N5,

20:52.000 --> 20:59.950
Chelsea Play at Stamford Bridge, Fulham Road, SW6,

21:00.000 --> 21:04.950
and Tottenham Hotspur Play at White Heart Lane,

21:05.000 --> 21:08.950
High Road, N17.

21:09.000 --> 21:15.950
To watch the very English game of cricket, go to Lord's Cricket Ground,

21:16.000 --> 21:20.950
in St John's Wood, NWA.

21:21.000 --> 21:26.000
This is the home of cricket for most English people.

21:35.000 --> 21:45.950
CHAPTER 8

21:46.000 --> 21:50.950
Museums and Galleries

21:51.000 --> 21:56.950
The British Museum in Great Russell Street is the biggest museum in Britain.

21:57.000 --> 21:59.950
Tickets are free.

22:00.000 --> 22:04.950
The Museum of London, at 150 London Wall,

22:05.000 --> 22:08.950
is one of the most interesting museums in the city.

22:09.000 --> 22:13.950
It tells the story of London and its people.

22:14.000 --> 22:21.950
One more museum that tells a story is the Museum of the Moving Image, Momi,

22:22.000 --> 22:26.950
on the South Bank, under Waterloo Bridge.

22:27.000 --> 22:34.950
This tells the story of cinema and television, and there are many things for visitors to see and do.

22:35.000 --> 22:38.950
You can act with actors on a film set,

22:39.000 --> 22:43.950
or you can read the news on TV.

22:44.000 --> 22:49.950
Four million people visit the National Gallery in Trafalgar Square every year.

22:50.000 --> 22:53.950
They come to look at more than 2,000 pictures.

22:54.000 --> 22:56.950
Tickets are free.

22:57.000 --> 23:03.950
At the National Portrait Gallery in St Martin's Place, you can see pictures of famous people.

23:04.000 --> 23:07.950
Tickets are free here, too.

23:08.000 --> 23:13.950
The London Dungeon in Thule Street is a Museum of Horror.

23:14.000 --> 23:17.950
Half a million people visit it every year,

23:18.000 --> 23:23.000
but they don't always stay to see it all.

23:24.000 --> 23:30.950
Madame Tussauds in Marlabin Road is famous for its people made from wax.

23:31.000 --> 23:38.950
You can see famous people from the past and famous people of today, Abraham Lincoln,

23:39.000 --> 23:44.950
Mahatma Gandhi, Nelson Mandela, Pavarotti,

23:45.000 --> 23:52.950
Marilyn Monroe, Charlie Chaplin, Sylvester Stallone,

23:53.000 --> 24:00.000
and in the Chamber of Horrors, you can see some very bad people.

24:20.000 --> 24:21.950
Chapter 9.

24:22.000 --> 24:24.950
Places and parades.

24:25.000 --> 24:27.950
The City.

24:28.000 --> 24:31.950
Londoners often talk about the city.

24:32.000 --> 24:36.950
C-Map on pages 18 and 19.

24:37.000 --> 24:43.950
They are talking about the oldest part of London, the home of the Bank of England,

24:44.000 --> 24:47.950
and many other big offices.

24:48.000 --> 24:55.950
About 5,000 people live in the city, and at weekends it feels empty.

24:56.000 --> 25:01.950
But between Monday and Friday, nearly half a million people come here

25:02.000 --> 25:04.950
to work in the banks and offices.

25:05.000 --> 25:11.950
Look for the city men, with their dark suits and umbrellas.

25:12.000 --> 25:16.950
St Paul's Cathedral is in the middle of the city,

25:17.000 --> 25:22.950
and the Bank of England has an interesting museum that you can visit.

25:23.000 --> 25:30.950
Also in the city is a very tall building, 60.6 metres high,

25:31.000 --> 25:33.950
called the Monument.

25:34.000 --> 25:41.950
Christopher Wren built this too, and it stands on the place where the fire of London began

25:42.000 --> 25:45.000
in 1666.

25:46.000 --> 25:50.950
Some interesting and exciting days.

25:51.000 --> 25:55.950
Every year, on a Saturday morning in June,

25:56.000 --> 26:02.950
foot guards and horse guards have a parade for the Queen.

26:03.000 --> 26:06.950
This is called Trooping the Colour.

26:07.000 --> 26:11.950
The colour is the flag that the soldiers carry.

26:12.000 --> 26:19.950
Thousands of people stand in the mall to see the Queen and the soldiers go past.

26:20.000 --> 26:27.950
The exciting Notting Hill Carnival is on the last Sunday and Monday in August.

26:28.000 --> 26:31.950
There are two wonderful parades to watch,

26:32.000 --> 26:36.950
one on Sunday and one on Monday.

26:37.000 --> 26:39.950
And you can see them going through the streets

26:40.000 --> 26:45.950
near Portobello Road and Ladbrook Grove.

26:46.000 --> 26:52.950
On the second Saturday in November, Londoners can see their new Lord Mayor

26:53.000 --> 26:55.950
in the Lord Mayor's show.

26:56.000 --> 27:02.950
A parade from Manchin House, the Lord Mayor's home, to the Strand.

27:03.000 --> 27:09.950
The Lord Mayor is the most important person in the city after the Queen.

27:10.000 --> 27:16.950
The first Mayor of London was Henry Fitzallen in 1189.

27:17.000 --> 27:23.950
They were not called Lord Mayor's until the time of King Henry VIII.

27:24.000 --> 27:31.950
Big red buses, London policemen, Buckingham Palace,

27:32.000 --> 27:38.950
Speakers Corner, Big Ben, Notting Hill Carnival.

27:39.000 --> 27:43.950
These are some of the things you can find in London.

27:44.000 --> 27:46.950
But there are many, many more.

27:47.000 --> 27:49.000
Come and see.

28:06.000 --> 28:09.000
Thank you.
